On Sat, 17 Jan 2004, Wade Renn wrote:

> I'm new to alphas so bear with me...
>
> I got an old alpha in return for some work and I am trying to install
> OpenBSD on it but I have a question about the partitioning portion.
>
> I boot up and start the install and print the partition table.  I
> deleted off the old partitions because this guys was running redhat and
> I had to modify the c: partition to reflect the entire scsi disk.  I put
> the c: partition at the 0 offset and then I got to add the a: partition
> but I am confused where to put the offset since the install program
> wants to start it off at 0 again.  Do I need to sit down and calculate
> where the partitions need to be or do I just start the a: partition from
> the 0 offset?

Hello,

I'm not sure if you are all that familiar with the Openbsd i386
partitioning, but the C partition is the WHOLE DISK, and you cannot change
it. The b partition is the swap partition, and with alpha it is easy to
partition, I usually set it to be 2x the physical memory of the box. Then
set the 'a' partition to be the rest of the disk..


Now with i386 you can do it either way, with alpha you must do the math fo
r what's left of the swap space.. so it's easiest to do it the way i
described.. let me know if you have any more questions.


	thanks

			ericj Jackson

